Difference between setTimeout, setImmediate, process.nextTick

In Node.js, these three differ based on when they execute in the event loop.

process.nextTick runs immediately after the current call stack, before the event loop continues. It executes before promises, timers, and I/O, so overusing it can block the event loop.

setTimeout schedules a callback in the timers phase after the specified delay, so it runs after microtasks and I/O polling.

setImmediate runs in the check phase, which is right after I/O callbacks, and is useful when we want to execute logic once I/O is completed.

So in short:

nextTick → highest priority

setTimeout → scheduled by time

setImmediate → after I/O

